    35	\title{Local Complete-Graph Optimality at Fitness Two and\\
    36	Strong-Selection Rigidity under Death--Birth Updating}
    37	\author{Alec Kriebel\\Independent Researcher\\
    38	\href{mailto:me@aleckriebel.com}{me@aleckriebel.com}\\
    39	\href{https://orcid.org/0009-0001-9320-500X}{ORCID 0009-0001-9320-500X}}
    40	\date{August 22, 2026}
    41	
    42	\begin{document}
    43	\maketitle
    44	
    45	\begin{abstract}
    46	We study fixation from a uniformly random single mutant under death--Birth
    47	updating on finite loopless directed weighted population structures.  After
    48	normalizing each target's incoming weights, the dynamics are parametrized by
    49	a loopless row-stochastic replacement kernel; let $J_n$ denote the uniform
    50	complete kernel.  We prove two complementary extremality results.  First, at
    51	mutant fitness two, $J_n$ is a strict nondegenerate local maximizer in the
    52	full normalized kernel polytope for every $n\ge3$.  This includes arbitrary
    53	directed and nonreversible perturbations.  The proof represents
    54	the fixation committor as a completely alternating coverage function of a
    55	fair-geometric union dual, converts fixation into a stationary collision
    56	observable, and decomposes the inverse-mean Hessian into standard,
    57	symmetric-balanced, and antisymmetric-balanced permutation sectors with
    58	positive sector certificates valid for every population size, using exact
    59	rational computer assistance over an explicitly finite range.
    60	Second, we close the complete-support case left outside the prior strongly
    61	connected noncomplete-support obstruction.  The leading
    62	strong-selection deficit is an explicit sum of squared discrepancies within
    63	incoming columns, with equality exactly at $J_n$ after normalization.
    64	Together with the prior obstruction and a source-component bound,
    65	this shows that no fixed finite directed weighting is a strict amplifier for
    66	every beneficial fitness.  We also prove
    67	global all-beneficial-fitness maximality for positive weighted triangles and
    68	within two symmetric weighted $K_4$ families.  These results establish strict local
    69	rigidity at fitness two and fixed-graph rigidity at strong selection, but
    70	not global complete-graph maximality at fitness two.
    71	\end{abstract}
    72	
    73	\noindent\textbf{Keywords.}
    74	death--Birth updating; fixation probability; evolutionary graph theory;
    75	coverage duality; local optimality; strong selection.
    76	
    77	\medskip
    78	\noindent\textbf{Mathematics Subject Classification (2020).}
    79	92D15; 60J10; 05C81.
    80	
    81	\section*{Author summary}
    82	
    83	Spatial structure can make a beneficial mutation more or less likely to take
    84	over a finite population.  For death--Birth updating, a natural unresolved
    85	question is whether the well-mixed population is globally extremal at fitness
    86	two.  We prove two complementary pieces of that picture.  At fitness two,
    87	the well-mixed kernel is strictly locally optimal against every infinitesimal
    88	perturbation of its normalized replacement kernel, including directed and
    89	nonreversible ones.  In the strong-selection limit, every
    90	fixed finite structure is eventually suppressing unless its replacement
    91	dynamics are exactly well mixed.  The local result comes from an exact ancestry process:
    92	fixation becomes a coverage probability, then a stationary collision rate,
    93	whose curvature splits into three symmetry types.  The second result comes
    94	from a sum of squares measuring unequal competitors for the same death event.
    95	Weighted triangles provide a global bridge: every nonuniform positive
    96	triangle suppresses fixation at every beneficial fitness.  The results do not
    97	show that the complete graph is globally best at fitness two or exclude graph
    98	sequences whose size threshold depends on fitness.
    99	
