--- FILE: sections/01_introduction.tex
     1	\section{Introduction}
     2	
     3	The Moran process is a foundational finite-population model of selection and
     4	genetic drift \citep{Moran1958}.  On a structured population, the fixation
     5	probability of a new mutant depends on both selection and the geometry of
     6	replacement.  The complete graph is the well-mixed baseline, while directed
     7	and weighted evolutionary graphs can amplify or suppress selection under
     8	particular update rules and fitness ranges
     9	\citep{Lieberman2005,Shakarian2012,Masuda2009,Allen2017,Allen2020}.
    10	Birth--death and death--Birth updating behave especially differently
    11	\citep{Kaveh2015}: the latter exposes every mutant to a uniformly sampled
    12	death event before local competition, which severely constrains
    13	amplification \citep{Hindersin2015,Tkadlec2020}.
    14	Transient dB amplifiers have since been studied through exact computation and
    15	guided spectral edge deletion \citep{Allen2020,Richter2023}.
    16	
    17	For a loopless directed weighting, multiply all incoming weights at a fixed
    18	target by the same positive number.  This changes no death--Birth transition.
    19	The intrinsic object is therefore a loopless row-stochastic replacement
    20	kernel $P$, with a row indexed by the dead target and a column by a potential
    21	parent.  Let $J_n$ be the uniform off-diagonal kernel and write
    22	\[
    23	 D_n(P,r)=\rho_{\dB}(J_n,r)-\rho_{\dB}(P,r)                 \tag{1.1}\label{1.1}
    24	\]
    25	for the uniformly initialized fixation deficit.  Global nonnegativity of
    26	$D_n(P,2)$ for every $n$ and every kernel induced by an undirected weighting
    27	remains open.
    28	This paper resolves two transverse rigidity problems for the same surface
    29	$D_n(P,r)$.
    30	
    31	First, we approach the complete kernel in structure space while holding
    32	fitness fixed at two.  For every $n\ge3$ and every nonzero loopless
    33	row-zero perturbation $\delta$,
    34	\[
    35	 D_n(J_n+\varepsilon\delta,2)
    36	   =\kappa_n(\delta)\varepsilon^2+O(\varepsilon^3),
    37	 \qquad \kappa_n(\delta)>0.                                \tag{1.2}\label{1.2}
    38	\]
    39	Thus $J_n$ is a strict nondegenerate local maximizer among all positive
    40	loopless row-stochastic replacement kernels.  This is stronger than a
    41	reversible or undirected statement: column imbalance and directed circulation
    42	are both allowed.  It is nevertheless genuinely local.  Its neighborhood
    43	may depend on $n$, and it does not constrain a sequence that stays far from
    44	$J_n$.  To our knowledge, no previous result establishes this strict local
    45	maximality in the full directed normalized kernel space.
    46	
    47	Second, we hold an arbitrary finite structure fixed and let $r\to\infty$.
    48	On complete directed support,
    49	\[
    50	 D_n(P,r)=\frac{\calE_{\rm dir}(P)}{n^2(n-2)r}+O(r^{-2}),   \tag{1.3}\label{1.3}
    51	\]
    52	where $\calE_{\rm dir}$ is an explicit incoming-column sum of squares.  On
    53	the normalized kernel space its zero set is exactly $P=J_n$; at the level of
    54	unnormalized weights, this is the class dynamically equivalent to $J_n$.
    55	Theorem~1 of \citet{Tkadlec2020} applies to every fixed
    56	strongly connected noncomplete population structure, including directed and
    57	weighted supports.  Thus complete directed support with unequal normalized
    58	weights is the residual strongly connected case.  Equation~\eqref{1.3}
    59	closes it sharply; together with the cited theorem and a source-component
    60	bound for non-strong supports, it rules out one fixed finite directed
    61	weighting that amplifies for every $r>1$.
    62	
    63	\begin{figure}[t]
    64	\centering
    65	\begin{tikzpicture}[>=Latex,scale=1]
    66	  \draw[->,thick] (0,0)--(7.5,0) node[right] {$r$};
    67	  \draw[->,thick] (0,0)--(0,3.7) node[above] {distance from $J_n$};
    68	  \fill (2.3,0) circle (2pt) node[below=4pt] {$r=2$};
    69	  \draw[very thick,blue!65!black] (2.3,0.15)--(2.3,1.45);
    70	  \draw[densely dashed,blue!35!black] (2.3,1.45)--(2.3,3.1);
    71	  \node[align=right,anchor=east,font=\small,blue!55!black] at (2.05,2.45)
    72	    {strict local maximum\\in every normalized direction};
    73	  \draw[very thick,red!70!black,->] (4.45,2.15)--(7.05,2.15);
    74	  \node[align=center,font=\small,red!60!black] at (5.75,2.65)
    75	    {fixed-structure\\strong-selection rigidity};
    76	  \node[align=center,font=\small] at (4.3,0.65)
    77	    {global all-$P$ statement at $r=2$ remains open};
    78	\end{tikzpicture}
    79	\caption{The two proved axes of the same complete-kernel deficit.  The
    80	fitness-two theorem is local in the full normalized structure space and valid for every
    81	population size; the strong-selection theorem is global in fixed finite
    82	structure and asymptotic in fitness.}
    83	\label{fig:axes}
    84	\end{figure}
    85	
    86	The mechanism behind \eqref{1.2} is different from ordinary weak-selection
    87	perturbation.  Branching and coalescing genealogical duals under selection
    88	have a substantial population-genetic history
    89	\citep{KroneNeuhauser1997,EtheridgeGriffiths2009,Mano2009}; related spatial
    90	dB work constructs the process and its dual on integer lattices
    91	\citep{Foo2024}.  Here we specialize this framework to arbitrary finite
    92	directed kernels at $r=2$: the update is an additive Boolean OR of a
    93	fair-geometric number of potential parents.
    94	Reversing the graphical maps produces a finite union-valued ancestry chain;
    95	no independent-lineage approximation is made.  Its stationary law represents
    96	the fixation committor as a completely alternating coverage function, and a
    97	marked one-sample factorization turns the stationary mean ancestral-set size
    98	into a collision probability.  At $J_n$,
    99	the first variation vanishes by rank projection.  The Hessian is an
   100	$S_n$-invariant quadratic form on all loopless row-zero matrices.  The
   101	tangent space splits without multiplicity into standard,
   102	symmetric-balanced, and antisymmetric-balanced sectors.  We prove all three
   103	scalars positive, for every population size, by rank-recursion and
   104	phase-contraction certificates.  The
   105	standard sector is exactly the column-imbalance direction and requires a
   106	separate signed three-channel argument.
   107	
   108	The global low-order results sharpen the picture.  Every nonuniform positive
   109	weighted triangle is a strict suppressor for every $r>1$.  Two maximally
   110	symmetric weighted $K_4$ families obey the same conclusion.  These theorems
   111	are not inferred from local curvature; they follow from independent exact
   112	sum-of-squares certificates.
   113	
   114	An earlier unrefereed research release established the strong-selection and
   115	low-order portions of this argument \citep{Kriebel2026Archive}.  This
   116	manuscript incorporates and revises those proofs while adding the fitness-two
   117	theory.  The following ledger makes that provenance explicit.
   118	
   119	\begin{center}
   120	\small
   121	\begin{tabular}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.45\linewidth}
   122	                    >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{0.47\linewidth}@{}}
   123	\toprule
   124	Component & Status relative to the earlier release\\
   125	\midrule
   126	Complete-support strong-selection expansion and fixed-graph closure
   127	  & Previously released; proof revised and integrated here.\\
   128	Undirected support limit and support-degree consequence
   129	  & Previously released; proof revised and integrated here.\\
   130	Weighted triangles and the two symmetric $K_4$ families
   131	  & Previously released; reorganized here.\\
   132	$r=2$ coverage representation and active collision identity
   133	  & First included here.\\
   134	Full directed Hessian decomposition and all-$n$ positivity of its three sectors
   135	  & First included here.\\
   136	Comparison of the local and strong-selection quantifier axes
   137	  & First included here.\\
   138	\bottomrule
   139	\end{tabular}
   140	\end{center}
   141	
   142	The quantifiers matter.  The fixed-graph theorem proves
   143	\[
   144	 \forall P\ \exists r_0(P)\ \forall r>r_0(P):\quad D_n(P,r)>0
   145	\]
   146	unless $P=J_n$.  It does not rule out a single
   147	fitness-independent sequence $(P_n)$ satisfying, for each fixed $r$,
   148	$D_n(P_n,r)<0$ once $n$ is sufficiently large.  Such growing-family
   149	quantifiers are substantive: \citet{Svoboda2024} construct one family that is
   150	independent of fitness and whose sufficiently large members simultaneously
   151	amplify under both Bd and dB updating for every fixed $1<r<1.2$.  We keep
   152	this distinction explicit throughout.  Separate unrefereed companion work by
   153	the present author constructs a fitness-independent family whose sufficiently
   154	large members simultaneously amplify both rules for every fixed
   155	$1<r<R_{\rm hyb}$, where
   156	$R_{\rm hyb}>3/2$ and $R_{\rm hyb}\approx1.5028569128$
   157	\citep{Kriebel2026Hybrid}.  That bounded-interval construction does not
   158	resolve the all-$r$ growing-family question.
   159	
   160	The paper is organized as follows.  Section~\ref{sec:model-results} states
   161	the model and theorem suite.  Sections~\ref{sec:duality}--\ref{sec:hessian}
   162	prove the fitness-two local theorem.  Section~\ref{sec:strong} treats fixed
   163	structures at strong selection.  Section~\ref{sec:small} gives the global
   164	low-order classifications.  The appendices contain the exact phase
   165	certificates, the symmetric $K_4$ algebra, and a quantifier ledger.
