     1	\section{Implications, limitations, and reproducibility}
     2	
     3	\subsection{A necessary condition for asymptotic universal amplification}
     4	
     5	The fixed-graph result does not interchange $n$ and $r$.  We first record the
     6	monotonicity needed for a useful support condition on graph sequences.
     7	
     8	\begin{lemma}[fitness monotonicity]\label{lem:fitness-monotone}
     9	For every loopless row-stochastic replacement kernel $P$ and every initial
    10	mutant set, the dB fixation probability is nondecreasing in mutant fitness
    11	$r>0$.
    12	\end{lemma}
    13	
    14	\begin{proof}
    15	For $0\le x\le1$,
    16	\[
    17	 \frac{\partial f_r(x)}{\partial r}
    18	 =\frac{x(1-x)}{[1+(r-1)x]^2}\ge0,
    19	 \qquad
    20	 \frac{\partial f_r(x)}{\partial x}
    21	 =\frac{r}{[1+(r-1)x]^2}>0.
    22	\]
    23	Couple chains with $r\le r'$ by using the same dead target $v$ and the same
    24	uniform threshold $U$ at every update, declaring the replacement mutant when
    25	$U\le f_r(P_{vS})$.  If their current mutant sets satisfy $S\subseteq T$,
    26	then $P_{vS}\le P_{vT}$ and the two displayed monotonicities preserve the
    27	inclusion after the update.  Starting both chains from the same set, fixation
    28	of the $r$-chain therefore implies fixation of the $r'$-chain.
    29	\end{proof}
    30	
    31	Lemma~\ref{lem:fitness-monotone} and
    32	Proposition~\ref{prop:support-limit} give, for every finite connected
    33	undirected graph,
    34	\[
    35	 \rho_{\dB}(W,r)\le1-\frac1n\sum_i\frac1{s_i+1}.              \tag{7.1}\label{7.1}
    36	\]
    37	
    38	\begin{proposition}[support degree must diverge]\label{prop:degree}
    39	Let $W_n$ be connected undirected weighted graphs of order $n\to\infty$.
    40	If, for every fixed $r>1$, $W_n$ is eventually a dB amplifier, then
    41	\[
    42	 \frac1n\sum_i\frac1{s_i+1}\longrightarrow0.                \tag{7.2}\label{7.2}
    43	\]
    44	Consequently the support degree of a uniformly chosen vertex tends to
    45	infinity in probability.
    46	\end{proposition}
    47	
    48	\begin{proof}
    49	At fixed $R>1$, eventual amplification and \eqref{7.1} give
    50	\[
    51	 \limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac1n\sum_i\frac1{s_i+1}
    52	 \le\lim_{n\to\infty}\{1-\rho_{\dB}(J_n,R)\}=\frac1R.
    53	\]
    54	Let $R\to\infty$.  For fixed $K$, the fraction with $s_i\le K$ is at most
    55	$(K+1)$ times the average in \eqref{7.2}.
    56	\end{proof}
    57	
    58	This excludes uniformly bounded-support families, but not supports completed
    59	by arbitrarily weak positive edges, diffuse perturbations, mesoscopic
    60	modules, or singular population-dependent kernels.
    61	
    62	\subsection{What the local theorem does and does not say}
    63	
    64	Theorem~\ref{thm:local} proves strict negative fixation curvature in every
    65	nonzero normalized directed direction at fixed order.  In particular,
    66	neither column imbalance nor directed circulation escapes the local maximum.
    67	The theorem does not supply a radius uniform in $n$, global concavity along
    68	rays, or a path from an arbitrary kernel to $J_n$ on which fixation is monotone.  The
    69	global conjecture
    70	\[
    71	 \rho_{\dB}(P,2)\le\rho_{\dB}(J_n,2)                         \tag{7.3}\label{7.3}
    72	\]
    73	for every kernel $P$ induced by an undirected weighting remains open.
    74	
    75	\subsection{Exact verification and proof status}
    76	
    77	The archive contains independent exact implementations for the two theorem
    78	axes.  The strong-selection suite constructs literal subset-state chains,
    79	checks row normalization and complete-graph lumpability, differentiates the
    80	absorption equations, verifies the incoming-column sum of squares, and
    81	replays the triangle and $K_4$ certificates.  The fitness-two suite
    82	independently builds the marked and active chains, verifies the collision
    83	normalization, reconstructs the rank Poisson equation, checks the tangent
    84	decomposition, and replays all three irreducible-sector certificates.
    85	
    86	The standard sector has an analytic tail certificate for $N\ge10$ and exact
    87	rational closure for $2\le N\le9$.  The symmetric sector uses exact solves
    88	for $3\le N\le39$, exact rational phase margins for $40\le N\le287$, and a
    89	coefficient/discriminant certificate for $N\ge288$.  All arithmetic in these
    90	finite ranges is rational.  The verifiers discharge the finite
    91	computer-assisted portions; sampled numerical values are never used to
    92	extend a quantifier.
    93	
    94	The one-command replay is
    95	\begin{verbatim}
    96	./replay.sh
    97	\end{verbatim}
    98	from the paper package.  The package-level integration audit separately
    99	checks the complete active stationary law, the full tangent decomposition,
   100	all three physical sector normalizations, the strong-selection column identity, and
   101	conversion from inverse-mean to fixation curvature.
   102	
