     2	
     3	Let $P_\varepsilon=J_n+\varepsilon\delta$ with
     4	$\delta\in\calT_n$.  By linearity,
     5	\[
     6	 K(P_\varepsilon)=K_0+\varepsilon\Delta.                    \tag{4.1}\label{4.1}
     7	\]
     8	Because every off-diagonal entry of $J_n$ is $1/(n-1)$, each fixed
     9	$\delta$ admits $\varepsilon_0>0$ such that $P_\varepsilon$ remains strictly
    10	positive off the diagonal for $|\varepsilon|<\varepsilon_0$.  Thus all
    11	positive-kernel identities from Section~\ref{sec:duality} apply in the
    12	neighborhood used below.  All entries are analytic there, and $K_0$ is
    13	irreducible.  Put
    14	\[
    15	 q=H-c_0\one,\qquad
    16	 G=(I-K_0+\one\nu_0)^{-1}.                                  \tag{4.2}\label{4.2}
    17	\]
    18	
    19	\subsection{Vanishing first variation}
    20	
    21	Let $S$ average a function on $\mathcal Y_n$ over all active states of the
    22	same rank $|B|$.  Permutation symmetry gives $SK_0=K_0S$.  A direct average
    23	of either active move shows
    24	\[
    25	 S\Delta S=0.                                               \tag{4.3}\label{4.3}
    26	\]
    27	Indeed, a uniformly random $k$-subset of $V\setminus\{v\}$ contains a
    28	sample from any row with mean probability $k/N$; after retargeting the
    29	corresponding mean is $(k-1)/N$.  More explicitly, if $f_k$ is any rank
    30	function, the two branchwise perturbations at $(B,v)$ are
    31	\[
    32	 \frac{f_{k+1}-f_k}{2}\sum_{i\notin B}\delta_{vi},\qquad
    33	 \frac{f_{k-1}-f_k}{2k}
    34	   \sum_{w\in B}\sum_{i\in B\setminus\{w\}}\delta_{wi}.    \tag{4.3a}\label{4.3a}
    35	\]
    36	Under the uniform rank-$k$ orbit their respective row sums are
    37	\[
    38	 \frac{N-k}{N}\sum_{i\ne v}\delta_{vi}=0,\qquad
    39	 \frac{k-1}{N}\sum_{i\ne w}\delta_{wi}=0.                \tag{4.3b}\label{4.3b}
    40	\]
    41	This proves \eqref{4.3} without reversibility.  Because $q$ and $Gq$ are rank functions
    42	and $\nu_0=\nu_0S$,
    43	\[
    44	 \nu_0\Delta Gq=\nu_0S\Delta SGq=0.                         \tag{4.4}\label{4.4}
    45	\]
    46	
    47	Stationary perturbation of the finite chain gives
    48	\[
    49	 \nu(P_\varepsilon)q
    50	 =\varepsilon\nu_0\Delta Gq
    51	  +\varepsilon^2\nu_0\Delta G\Delta Gq+O(\varepsilon^3).
    52	                                                                    \tag{4.5}\label{4.5}
    53	\]
    54	Define
    55	\[
    56	 \Rtwo_{n}(\delta)=\nu_0\Delta G\Delta Gq.                    \tag{4.6}\label{4.6}
    57	\]
    58	Equations \eqref{3.12}, \eqref{3.17}, and \eqref{4.4}--\eqref{4.6}
    59	already prove expansion \eqref{2.6}; it remains to prove that
    60	$\Rtwo_{n}$ is positive definite on the full tangent space.
    61	
    62	\subsection{The three irreducible tangent sectors}
    63	
    64	For $\delta\in\calT_n$, let
    65	\[
    66	 s_j=\sum_i\delta_{ij},\qquad \sum_js_j=0,                   \tag{4.7}\label{4.7}
    67	\]
    68	and define the standard embedding
    69	\[
    70	 E(s)_{ij}=\frac{s_i+(n-1)s_j}{n(n-2)}\quad(i\ne j),
    71	 \qquad E(s)_{ii}=0.                                       \tag{4.8}\label{4.8}
    72	\]
    73	Then $E(s)\one=0$ and its column-sum vector is $s$.  Consequently
    74	$B=\delta-E(s)$ has zero row and column sums, and
    75	\[
    76	 \delta=E(s)+\frac{B+B^{\mathsf T}}2
    77	                +\frac{B-B^{\mathsf T}}2.                    \tag{4.9}\label{4.9}
    78	\]
    79	The three terms are Frobenius-orthogonal.  Their dimensions are
    80	\[
    81	 n-1,\qquad \frac{n(n-3)}2,\qquad
    82	 \frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2,                                      \tag{4.10}\label{4.10}
    83	\]
    84	which sum to $n(n-2)=\dim\calT_n$.  They are, respectively, the standard,
    85	symmetric row/column-balanced, and antisymmetric balanced irreducible
    86	$S_n$-modules.  At $n=3$ the symmetric module has dimension zero.
    87	
    88	For completeness, the symmetric off-diagonal matrices form the two-subset
    89	permutation module
    90	$\mathbf1\oplus[n-1,1]\oplus[n-2,2]$; its row-balanced kernel is
    91	$[n-2,2]$.  The antisymmetric balanced kernel is
    92	$\bigwedge^2[n-1,1]=[n-2,1,1]$, while $E(s)$ realizes $[n-1,1]$.
    93	Thus the three summands in \eqref{4.9} are pairwise nonisomorphic and occur
    94	with multiplicity one, including the stated $n=3$ degeneration.
    95	
    96	The quadratic form \eqref{4.6} is invariant under simultaneous relabeling of
    97	targets and sources.  Multiplicity-freeness therefore makes the three spaces
    98	orthogonal for $\Rtwo_{n}$ as well, and $\Rtwo_{n}$ acts by one scalar on each.
    99	The balanced subspace is exactly
   100	\[
   101	 \calB_n=\calS_n\oplus\calA_n,                              \tag{4.10b}\label{4.10b}
   102	\]
   103	where $\calS_n$ and $\calA_n$ denote the symmetric- and
   104	antisymmetric-balanced summands in \eqref{4.9}.  The standard module $E(s)$
   105	is precisely the complementary column-imbalance direction.
   106	
   107	\begin{theorem}[sector positivity]\label{thm:sectors}
   108	The standard scalar is positive for every $n\ge3$, the
   109	symmetric-balanced scalar is positive for every $n\ge4$, and the
   110	antisymmetric-balanced scalar is positive for every $n\ge3$.
   111	\end{theorem}
   112	
   113	The proof is in Appendix~\ref{app:sectors}.  We summarize its architecture.
   114	For the standard sector, a signed three-channel quotient separates positive
   115	and negative orientations.  Schur elimination turns each completed negative
   116	excursion into a nonnegative re-entry operator.  Exact first-phase barriers
   117	and a uniform row-sum contraction control the entire alternating resolvent.
   118	For the antisymmetric sector, a one-feature reduction sends a rank Poisson
   119	gradient through a positive decreasing recurrence; the second perturbation
   120	is then a sum of nonnegative sampling-without-replacement terms, at least one
   121	strict.  For the symmetric sector, the exact quotient has a good/bad block form
   122	\[
   123	 \begin{pmatrix}S&C\\-D&Q\end{pmatrix}                     \tag{4.11}\label{4.11}
   124	\]
   125	with $S,C,D,Q$ nonnegative.  Schur elimination converts every completed bad
   126	excursion into one factor of a nonnegative return operator $A$.  Explicit
   127	positive phase barriers dominate the alternating resolvent $(I+A)^{-1}$.
   128	The computer-assisted part is finite and exact: rational stationary solves
